 { Magnus Carlsen – Luke McShane, London Classic 2009 Learning objective: This game is an example of Black’s inability to create counterplay despite initially having control of the c-file. The first twenty moves of this game are irrelevant to this chapter, hence I leave them without comment. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Na6 8. Re1 Qe8 9. Bf1 c6 10. Rb1 Bg4 11. d5 c5 12. Be2 Kh8 13. a3 Bd7 14. b4 b6 15. Bg5 Ng8 16. Nb5 f6 17. Bh4 Qe7 18. Nd2 Nh6 19. Nf1 Rfc8 20. Ne3 Nc7 21. bxc5 Nxb5 ( { An interesting alternative was } 21... bxc5 $5 { ⩲ and this structure is studied in the next chapter. } ) 
22. cxb5 Rxc5 { We have reached the position of interest. The reader should examine this position very carefully and try to make an assessment of it. After analyzing this game, I would advise the reader to contrast his initial opinion and his final opinion. I imagine it might be quite different. In his analysis of this game, McShane wonders if going into this position } ( { with 21...Nxb5 } ) 
 { was the right decision. His original evaluation indicated that he would have sufficient counterplay with his rooks on the c-file, but as he points out, there are no entry points. This is a phenomenon which I have observed far too many times in my own experience, and in those games I have analyzed. Black’s position is reasonably good in appearance, but the truth is that it is very difficult to find counterplay, and although it is Black who is temporarily in control of the only open file, only White can really benefit from queenside play, as we will see in this game. } 23. f3 $1 { ⩲ A simple move that should be remembered. It provides extra support to the chain e4-d5, and it allows the bishop to come back to f2 in order to press against Black’s queenside. } ( { As McShane points out, } 23. a4 $2 { is a serious imprecision, since after } g5 24. Bg3 f5 { White is forced to destroy his ”glorious” e4-d5 chain with } 25. exf5 ( { but not } 25. f3 $2 f4 ) 
Nxf5 { . Now the d5-pawn has been weakened, while the bishop on g3 is temporarily useless. A possible continuation is } 26. Rc1 Rxc1 27. Qxc1 Rc8 28. Qd2 Rc5 { with level chances. } ) 
Rac8 24. Bd3 { Something to note is how White can improve slowly, while Black can’t. The move 24.Bd3 clears e2 for the queen, prepares against ...f6-f5, and gives extra protection to the c2-square. Meanwhile, Black controls the c-file, but cannot do much with it. } ( { Less accurate is } 24. Bf2 { since Black immediately gains counterplay with } f5 $1 { . A possible continuation is } 25. Bd3 fxe4 26. fxe4 Bf6 27. a4 Bg5 { and Black is closer to equality than in the game. } ) 
Qf8 { There is nothing better than spending a tempo to prepare ...f6-f5. White was planning to follow with a3-a4, Qe2, Nc4 and only then bring his bishop back to f2. Black cannot afford to wait with his queen on e7, as ...f6-f5 is his only constructive plan. } ( { Attempting to ”use” the c-file with } 24... Rc3 { does not really go anywhere after } 25. a4 { when White can continue with his plans, ignoring the rook on c3 } ( { but not } 24... Qf8 24. Rb3 $6 { since trading pieces alleviates Black’s space problems } ) 
 { and Black cannot insist with } Ra3 $2 { since the rook gets trapped after: } 26. Nc4 Rc3 ( { or } 26... Ra2 27. Qb3 $18 ) 
27. Rb4 $18 { Followed by Qd2 . } ) 
25. Bf2 { There is nothing else to do on h4, so the bishop comes to f2, aiming at the queenside. } f5 26. a4 { Getting ready to create a weakness in Black’s camp with a4-a5. } R5c7 ( { Black is not ready to close the position with } 26... f4 $2 { as White continues with the typical manoeuvre } 27. Nc2 R5c7 28. Nb4 { followed by Nc6 with a near-winning position. For example: } g5 29. h3 { (preventing ...g5-g4) } Ng8 30. Nc6 h5 31. a5 g4 32. axb6 axb6 33. Bxb6 Rb7 34. Ba7 $1 { Followed by b5-b6 and Ba6 winning, while Black’s kingside attack is not getting anywhere; and if } Bxc6 $6 35. dxc6 Rxa7 36. b6 { and the passed pawns decide the game. } ) 
27. h3 { This prophylactic move protects the g4-square, and prepares against a future ...f5-f4 followed by ...g6-g5-g4. } ( { If } 27. Nc2 { , to follow with Nb4-c6, then McShane was ready establish contact on the kingside with } g5 28. Nb4 g4 { with counterplay. } ) 
Bf6 28. Qd2 { Preparing a4-a5 to create a weakness on the queenside. } ( { If } 28. Nc2 { McShane intended to gain counterplay with } fxe4 29. fxe4 g5 { followed by ...g5-g4 with reasonable fighting chances. For example: } 30. g4 ( { or } 30. Qd2 g4 31. Be3 Ng8 32. hxg4 Bxg4 { with counterplay; } ) 
 ( { but not } 30. Qe2 $6 g4 31. h4 g3 $1 32. Bxg3 Qg7 33. Bf2 Rg8 { with excellent compensation for the pawn } ) 
Ng8 31. Nb4 Ne7 32. Nc6 Ng6 33. a5 Nf4 34. Bf1 h5 { With sharp tactical complications. } ) 
 ( { PH: An alternative I would consider is } 28. Qe2 { followed by putting the knight on c4 immediately. } ) 
Bg5 ( { If } 28... f4 $6 29. Nc2 g5 30. Nb4 Nf7 31. a5 $1 { ± and White’s attack arrives faster. PH: After 30.Nb4, Black has an interesting try in } g4 $5 31. hxg4 Bxg4 { , if White takes the piece then Black gets a strong attack with a knight on g4 and queen on h6. However, White can simply run away with } 32. Kf1 { . } ) 
29. a5 { Black must make a difficult decision between having a weak pawn on b6, or a weak pawn on a7. } fxe4 $6 ( { It was preferable to play } 29... bxa5 30. Qxa5 ( 29... fxe4 29. Ra1 $5 { ⩲ is safer } ) 
 { when Black’s position requires precision, but is quite playable. For example: } Qf6 31. Nc4 $5 { (threatening Bxa7) } fxe4 ( { but not } 31... Rxc4 $2 32. Bxc4 Rxc4 33. Qxa7 { and the passed b-pawn decides the game } ) 
32. fxe4 Bf4 $1 { And the position is very far from clear since } 33. Bxa7 $2 { is refuted by } Bxh3 $1 { with a winning attack. } ) 
30. fxe4 $6 ( { Better was } 30. axb6 $1 { forcing a weakness on b6 , rather than a7; } axb6 31. fxe4 $16 { is similar to the game. } ) 
Nf7 $6 { Once again allowing White to create a weakness on b6. } ( { The correct way was } 30... bxa5 31. Qxa5 Bf4 $14 { with a playable position for Black. } ) 
31. axb6 axb6 32. Qe2 Rb7 33. Nc4 $16 { And as I said ten moves ago, only White is going to benefit from queenside play. The weakness on b6 is crippling, and prevents Black from creating further threats on the kingside. The battle is strategically won; now let’s see how Carlsen utilizes his advantage. } Qd8 34. Rf1 Kg7 35. Kh1 { Again, White can slowly improve his position, while Black’s pieces are cramped and have little to do. } ( { As McShane points out, } 35. Bxb6 $2 { gives away most of the advantage after: } Rxb6 36. Qf2 ( { or } 36. Rxf7 Kxf7 37. Qf2 Kg7 38. Nxb6 Bh4 $1 39. g3 ( 39. Qe3 Bg5 40. Qf2 Bh4 $11 ) 
Bxg3 40. Qxg3 Qxb6 { with a drawn position } ) 
Rxb5 37. Qxf7 Kh6 $14 ) 
Be8 36. Qb2 Nh6 { And as usually happens, passive defence leads to mistakes. } ( { A better defence was } 36... Ra8 37. Ra1 $5 Rxa1 38. Rxa1 Bh4 39. Bg1 Qb8 40. Ra6 Bd8 41. Be3 $16 { when Black’s position is extremely difficult to hold. } ) 
 { Black missed the tactical shot: } 37. Bxb6 $1 Qe7 $1 { The only move to stay in the game. } ( { Weak is: } 37... Qd7 $2 38. Nxe5 $1 dxe5 39. Qxe5 Kg8 40. Bd4 $18 ) 
 ( { Black loses by force after: } 37... Rxb6 38. Qf2 $1 { (threatening Qf8# as well as Nxb6) } Bxb5 ( 38... Rxb5 $4 39. Qf8 ) 
39. Nxb6 $1 Bxd3 40. Nxc8 Bxf1 41. Qa7 $1 Kh8 42. Rb8 { With a winning attack, for example: } Ng8 ( 42... Bd3 $2 43. Nxd6 ) 
43. Rb7 Nf6 44. Nb6 { Followed by Rb8 . } ) 
38. Qf2 $6 { We are close to the time control on move 40, and Carlsen makes some imprecisions. } ( { The most accurate was } 38. Ba5 $1 Bxb5 ( { or } 38. Qf2 Rxb5 39. Bb4 Rd8 40. Nxd6 $1 { +- } ) 
39. Nxd6 Qxd6 40. Bxb5 { with a near-winning position. } ) 
Rcb8 39. Rb3 Ng8 { Aiming for ...Nf6-h5 with some counterplay. } 40. Be2 { Covering the h5-square. } Nf6 { And we have passed the time control. } 41. Bf3 Rxb6 { This is an interesting practical resource, but Carlsen manages to find a beautiful way to refute it. } ( { The alternative McShane suggests is } 41... Nd7 42. Ba7 Ra8 43. b6 Nc5 44. Rbb1 h5 45. Na5 Rbxa7 46. bxa7 Qxa7 47. Nc4 Qc7 { when Black’s defence is hard to break. } ) 
42. Nxb6 Qc7 43. h4 Bh6 { The knight on f6 is now defenceless. } 44. Na4 $1 { Threatening 45.Nc3, forcing Black to capture. } Rxb5 { But now: } 45. Be2 $1 Rxb3 46. Qxf6+ Kg8 47. Nc5 $1 { This is the key to White’s last four moves: the knight is immune, and Black’s position is near collapse due to the threats Ne6 and Bg4-e6. } Rg3 { Making things easier for White. } ( { If } 47... dxc5 $4 48. d6 Qf7 ( { or } 48... Qd7 49. Bc4 ) 
49. d7 $1 Qxd7 50. Bc4 { with forced mate. } ) 
 ( { Or: } 47... Qxc5 $4 48. Qe6 Kg7 49. Qe7 Kg8 50. Qxe8 Kg7 51. Qf8 ) 
 ( { The best defence was } 47... Rb2 { but after the forcing sequence } 48. Bg4 Bg7 49. Ne6 Qf7 50. Nxg7 Qxf6 51. Rxf6 Kxg7 52. Rxd6 { the endgame should be winning for White. } ) 
48. Ne6 Qf7 49. Qxf7+ Bxf7 50. Rb1 $1 Be8 ( { Suicidal is } 50... Bxe6 $2 51. dxe6 { when the threat is Rb8 followed by e6-e7-e8=Q: } Bf8 52. Rb8 Kg7 53. Rb7 Kf6 ( { or } 53... Kg8 54. e7 $18 ) 
54. Rf7 $18 ) 
51. Bf3 $18 { Black cannot defend without the help of his trapped rook, thus his position is lost. The rest of the game is interesting, but irrelevant to our chapter, so I give it without further comments. The reader may find some interesting annotations (by McShane) to the rest of this game in Chessbase Magazine. } Kf7 52. Rb7+ Kf6 53. Rxh7 Bf4 54. Nxf4 exf4 55. e5+ dxe5 56. d6 e4 57. Bxe4 Re3 58. Bd5 Kf5 59. Kh2 Re5 60. Bf3 Kf6 61. d7 { Final remarks 1. The option 21...bxc5 deserves serious consideration and the reader should study it carefully in the next chapter. 2. Although Black controlled the c-file, he was unable to use it. White was able to gradually gain control of the queenside thanks to his spatial advantage. 3. Since Black had nothing to gain on the queenside, all attention should be directed to kingside play. The break ...f6-f5 was absolutely necessary, and unlike many other King’s Indian positions, advancing ...f5-f4 was not always a good idea. In fact, on moves 26-29 it would have been a poor decision since this would slow down the creation of kingside threats, while White can create a strong queenside attack very quickly. In general, White’s queenside play can distract Black’s forces rather easily once the c-file is open. } 
 1-0

