 { Radoslaw Wojtaszek – Dariusz Swiercz, Warsaw 2011 Learning objective: This game reinforces the idea that White is ultimately the one who can gain control of the c-file, despite Black having extra moves. In particular, the reader should notice Black’s lack of targets. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. g3 d5 4. Bg2 Bb4+ 5. Nd2 O-O 6. Nf3 dxc4 7. Qc2 Nc6 8. Qxc4 Bd6 $6 { This opening imprecision allows White to gain a firm control of the centre. } ( { Better was } 8... Qd5 9. O-O Bxd2 10. Qxd5 exd5 11. Bxd2 Re8 12. e3 Bf5 { with reasonable chances for Black. } ) 
9. O-O Qe7 10. e4 e5 11. d5 Nb8 12. Qc2 Nbd7 13. Nc4 Nb6 { We have reached an interesting position. White has a notable advantage because of his good control of the centre, and the backward c7-pawn, but how shall he take advantage of these factors? } 14. Nxd6 $1 cxd6 { And the resulting position is the beginning of our study. We have reached the structure of this chapter, and this is the moment to assess the position. At first sight it might seem that Black is doing completely fine, as he may soon start to fight for the c-file with ...Bd7 and ...Rac8. Moreover, Black’s dark-squared bishop (the bad bishop) has already been traded so he should have nothing to worry about. Unfortunately for Black, reality is not so simple. The biggest problem is that, as in the previous game, White has a significant space advantage due to the favourable e4-d5 vs e5-d6 chain. Despite Black’s temporary activity, White will be able to reorganize his pieces more efficiently and emerge with a dominating position. } 15. a4 $1 { A good continuation, to proceed with a4-a5 gaining space. } Bg4 ( { If } 15... a5 { preventing a4-a5, the weird-looking } 16. Nh4 $5 { prevents Bg4xf3 thus putting Black in a difficult position as he cannot develop his bishop. } Qd8 ( { but not } 16... Bd7 $2 17. Qb3 $1 { winning a pawn } ) 
 ( { or } 16... Bg4 $2 17. f3 $1 Bh5 18. Qb3 Nc8 19. Nf5 Qc7 20. Bd2 { followed with Rac1 with domination } ) 
17. b3 Bd7 18. Ba3 Nc8 19. Rfc1 $16 { Black’s forces are against the wall due to their lack of space. } ) 
 ( { If } 15... Bd7 { then } 16. b3 Rac8 17. Qd3 $14 { gives White a promising advantage due to his extra space and the weak d6-pawn. } ) 
16. Nd2 $5 { This is an interesting approach. Wojtaszek begins the knight transfer to c4 immediately, as he knows Black’s pieces do not have targets to attack. } ( { A worthy option was } 16. a5 Nbd7 ( { or } 16. Nd2 Bxf3 17. Bxf3 Nbd7 18. b4 $14 { when White’s bishops are very strong despite the closed nature of the position } ) 
17. b4 $14 { with a significant space advantage. } ) 
Be2 { Black is simply ”hitting the air”. } ( { If } 16... Rac8 17. Qd3 { White has a very pleasing advantage. An illustrative continuation is } Qd7 18. b3 Bh3 19. a5 Na8 20. Nc4 Nc7 21. f4 $1 { ± with a strong positional advantage. } ) 
 ( { Another option was } 16... Rfc8 17. Qd3 Nbd7 18. Qe3 { and Black really has nothing to aim for. } a5 19. b3 Nc5 20. Nc4 Qc7 21. Ba3 b6 22. Rac1 $16 { and Black has no good counterplay against the positional threat of f2-f4. } ) 
17. Re1 Rac8 { Black really does seem to have some initiative, but this is only a facade. } 18. Qb1 { The queen is awkwardly placed, but this is only temporary. } Bh5 ( { The bishop becomes an easy target after } 18... Ba6 $2 19. b4 $1 { threatening b4-b5. } Bc4 20. Bh3 Rc7 21. Ra3 { Threatening Rc3 followed by a4-a5, and after } Nfd7 22. Rc3 Rfc8 23. a5 Na8 24. Nxc4 Rxc4 25. Rxc4 Rxc4 26. Be3 { White has a winning positional advantage; this is very similar to Polugaevsky – Ivkov from Chapter 11. } ) 
 ( { Or } 18... Bc4 $2 19. b3 Ba6 20. b4 { transposing into the variation above. } ) 
19. a5 Nbd7 { Black’s pieces slowly go back to their passive positions. } ( 19... Nc4 20. Nf1 $5 { White keeps as many pieces on the board as possible, while threatening b2-b3 trapping the knight } ( { also possible is } 19... Nbd7 19. Qd3 Nxd2 21. Bxd2 { with excellent chances for White } ) 
b6 21. axb6 Nxb6 ( { even worse is } 21... axb6 22. b3 Na5 23. Be3 $16 ) 
22. Qd3 Rc7 23. Ne3 $16 { Black has many weaknesses and there is no semblance of counterplay. } ) 
20. b4 { Covering the c5-square, and gaining more space. } Bg6 21. Qd3 { The queen is back on the board, and it becomes clear that Black did not accomplish anything during the last five moves. } Nh5 { Attempting to create counterplay with the usual ...f7-f5. } 22. Bh3 { A good decision, preventing Black’s counterplay and taking control of an important diagonal. } ( { Incorrect was } 22. Nf1 { due to } f5 $1 23. f3 $14 { when White preserves an advantage, but at least Black can aim for activity on the f-file. } ) 
Rc7 23. Nf1 Ndf6 24. f3 $16 { Note how the bishop on h3 prevents Black from doubling his rooks on the c-file. His position is already hopeless. } h6 25. Ne3 Nh7 26. Bd2 Ng5 27. Bg2 Qd7 28. Rac1 Rxc1 ( { Fighting for the c-file with } 28... Rfc8 { can be met by } 29. Rxc7 Rxc7 30. b5 $5 { gaining more space on the queenside, preparing Bb4 or b5-b6 or a5-a6, with a huge advantage. } ) 
29. Rxc1 Nh3+ ( { Instead } 29... Rc8 $2 { loses on the spot to } 30. Rxc8 Qxc8 31. Nc4 Qc7 32. f4 $1 { when Black is losing a piece after } Nh7 33. f5 ) 
30. Kf1 Ng5 ( { Again } 30... Rc8 { runs into a nice tactical refutation: } 31. Rxc8 Qxc8 32. Qc4 $1 Qd7 ( { or } 32... Qxc4 33. Nxc4 Ng5 34. Nxd6 $18 ) 
33. a6 $1 bxa6 34. Qc6 $1 { When the knight on h3 is hanging and } Qxc6 $2 { fails to } 35. dxc6 Ng5 36. c7 ) 
31. Qc2 $18 { White has gained undisputed control of the c-file, and Black still has no counterplay; the rest is easy. } a6 32. Ke1 Qb5 33. Qc4 Qa4 34. h4 Nxe4 { A desperate hope for counterplay. } ( { Or } 34... Nh7 35. Kf2 { followed by Qc7 taking the queenside pawns and winning. } ) 
35. fxe4 Nxg3 36. Kf2 Nh5 37. b5 $1 { Offering to trade queens to clarify the position. } Qa3 38. Bb4 Qb2+ 39. Rc2 Qb1 40. Bxd6 { Final remarks 1. White’s decision to modify the structure with 14. Nxd6 was fully justified. Wojtaszek was able to see into the future of the game and realize that the c-file would ultimately belong to White, while Black would not be able to gain any counterplay. 2. A key move for White was 22. Bh3, preventing Black’s counterplay with ...f7-f5. After this move Black falls into a passive and rather desperate position. 3. The reader should see a close similarity between this position and the games Polugaevsky – Ivkov and Smeets – Tiviakov from Chapter 11, in which White inevitably gains control of the c-file due to his spatial advantage. } 
 1-0

