 { White’s endgame prospects If White manages to expand on the queenside with a2-a4-a5 or something similar, then even after trading all major pieces and some minor pieces, he should have excellent winning chances due to his spatial edge. The following fragment is an example. The game Beliavsky – Carlhammar, Gibraltar 2009, reached the position in the diagram. White has expanded on the queenside already, securing a significant spatial advantage. } 23. Bf1 $5 { White is willing to simplify the position to head for an endgame he considers to be winning. } ( { White could have also fought for the c-file after: } 23. Qd3 Rdc7 24. Rc2 Nd7 25. Rdc1 $16 { When Black needs high precision to stay in the game. For example, } Ne7 $2 ( { better was } 25. Bf1 Ngf8 ) 
 { loses due to: } 26. Bb6 $1 Nxb6 27. axb6 Rd7 28. Bb5 $1 { And after } Rxc2 29. Rxc2 axb5 30. Qxb5 { the rook on d7 is hanging, and there is nothing better than } Nc6 31. dxc6 bxc6 32. Rxc6 { . } ) 
Rdc7 24. Nd2 Rxc1 ( { If } 24... b5 { White is much better after } 25. axb6 $1 Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Rxc1 27. Qxc1 Bxb6 28. Bxb6 Qxb6 29. Qb2 $5 { since the weak a6-pawn will be a problem sooner or later. } ) 
25. Rxc1 Rxc1 26. Qxc1 Qc7 27. Qxc7 Bxc7 { Trading pieces is supposed to alleviate Black’s spatial problem, but here this will not be enough. } 28. h4 $1 { Threatening the decisive Bh3-c8 attacking the b7-pawn. } Nf8 ( { A better defence was } 28... Kf8 { though after } 29. Bh3 Ne7 30. Kg2 Ke8 31. Kf3 Kd8 32. Nc4 Nd7 33. g4 $5 { White will expand on the kingside with excellent winning chances. } ) 
29. Bh3 N8d7 ( 29... N6d7 { does not help: } 30. Nc4 f6 ( { or } 30... Kh7 31. Bb6 $1 Nxb6 ( 31... Bxb6 32. axb6 $18 ) 
32. axb6 Bb8 33. Bc8 $18 ) 
31. h5 $1 { Fixing another weakness. } Kf7 32. Kg2 Ke7 33. Bf5 Kf7 { And now White has many winning methods, such as } 34. Bb6 $1 Bb8 35. Ne3 Ke7 36. Be6 $1 Nxe6 37. Nf5 Kf7 38. dxe6 Kxe6 39. Nxg7 Kf7 40. Nf5 { followed by Nxh6 . } ) 
30. g4 $1 { Gaining even more space. } Nb8 31. g5 hxg5 32. hxg5 Nfd7 33. Nc4 Kf8 34. Kf1 Ke7 35. Ke2 Kd8 36. f4 $1 { +- The decisive break. Black will be unable to resist the opening of more lines due to his poor piece placement, his weaknesses and his lack of space. } f6 ( { White is also winning after } 36... exf4 37. Bxf4 Ke7 38. Be3 Kd8 39. Bd4 g6 40. Kf3 Ke7 41. Kf4 { with the threat of e4-e5. Black will fall into zugzwang sooner or later. } ) 
37. Kf3 Nf8 ( { Or } 37... fxg5 38. fxe5 Nxe5 39. Nxe5 dxe5 40. Bxg5 Ke8 41. Bc8 { and wins. } ) 
38. gxf6 gxf6 39. fxe5 fxe5 40. Bg5+ Ke8 41. Bc8 { And the b7-pawn is finally lost. } Nfd7 42. Bxb7 { Final remarks 1. I would like to emphasize that White’s plan of trading rooks and queens with 23. Bf1 was not the only way to proceed. The endgame was far superior, but victory was not completely guaranteed. 2. It would have been interesting for White to first press in the middlegame a little longer with 23. Qd3, as I believe Black did not really have the resources to maintain control of the c-file had White found the right continuation. } 
 1-0

