 { How should Black play? White’s previous three wins were very convincing, and the reader may wonder how Black is supposed to play. I want to answer this question by first showing a short fragment from the game Bouaziz – Beliavsky, Szirak 1987. After a Ruy Lopez opening, the game reached the position in the diagram, and now Black turned into the structure we are studying with: } 1... cxd5 2. cxd5 { And now: } Ng6 $1 { This is a good move for two reasons. Black will improve his least-useful piece by playing ...Be7-d8-b6 while the knight on g6 is ready to jump to f4. Note how difficult it is for White to cover all the knight jumps when Black’s knights are on f6 and g6; it seems like Black always has something useful to do. } ( { A different continuation, such as } 18... bxa4 19. bxa4 Rb8 20. Qe2 a5 21. Ne3 Ng6 22. g3 Rec8 23. Bd2 $14 { , gives White a dominating position after Bd3 and Nc4 . Once again Black lacks queenside targets. } ) 
3. axb5 $2 ( { PH: This is actually a big mistake. } 3. g3 { keeps the tension favorably. } ) 
axb5 4. Bd2 Be7 5. Qa1 ( { If } 21. Bd3 Nf4 $5 ( 21. Qa1 Bd8 { might be too slow, and after } 22. g3 Qb8 23. Qe2 Bb6 24. Ne3 { White secures a stable position with prospects for an advantage } ) 
 { Black has good counterplay after } 22. Bxf4 exf4 23. Qd2 Qc3 $1 { with a dynamic balance. } ) 
 ( 21. g3 $5 { deserves attention too. } ) 
Bd8 6. Rc1 Qb8 7. Bd3 ( { Of course } 23. Ra7 $2 { simply cooperates with Black, and after } Bb6 24. Ra2 Bg4 25. Ne1 Nf4 $1 { ∓ Black has serious kingside threats. } ) 
Bb6 { It took five moves to bring this bishop from the useless f8-square to the powerful b6-square; it was worth it. This bishop covers the entry points a7 and c7, and exerts pressure against White’s kingside. } 8. Rxc8 ( { Black is at least equal after } 24. Be3 Rxc1 25. Qxc1 Rc8 26. Qa1 Nf4 27. Bxf4 exf4 ) 
Rxc8 9. g3 $6 ( { Better is } 25. h3 Nh5 26. Ne3 Ngf4 27. Bf1 { with a level game. } ) 
Ng4 10. Be1 $6 ( { White is only slightly worse after: } 26. Ne3 Nxe3 27. Bxe3 Bxe3 28. fxe3 Bg4 $5 ) 
f5 $1 { ⩱ Black’s dreams come true. Now he is able to undermine White’s central pawn structure without having to lose control of the open file on the queenside. I should say White did not play well enough, and in practice Black will rarely be able to accomplish such an ideal position. Nevertheless, it is good for the reader to know what to hope for. Black had a significant advantage after: } 11. exf5 Ne7 12. Nh4 Nxd5 13. Ng6 Ngf6 14. Qd1 Qb7 15. g4 Be8 16. Ng3 Nf4 { The rest of the game is irrelevant to our purposes. Final remarks 1. It should be noted that, unlike the previous three examples, Black transferred into this pawn structure under good conditions. 2. The conditions were good because there was time for the plan ...Be7-d8-b6 to be executed, and because Black’s knights were well placed on f6 and g6. In addition, White was not in time to claim queenside space with a2-a4-a5. The following two games are more recent and much more realistic executions of Black’s plans. } 
 0-1

