 { Ruslan Ponomariov – Teimour Radjabov, Medias 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates Black’s chances for kingside play based on the ...f7-f5 break. } 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Nge2 e5 7. Bg5 c6 8. Qd2 Nbd7 9. d5 h6 10. Be3 ( { The pawn is immune as } 10. Bxh6 { is met by } Nxe4 $1 11. Nxe4 Qh4 12. g3 Qxh6 13. Qxh6 Bxh6 { with approximately level chances. } ) 
cxd5 11. cxd5 { We have reached the position of interest. The two earlier games should have been enough to convince the reader that White’s spatial advantage is quite promising. Black needs to find a concrete way to react or he will be dominated as in the earlier examples. } a6 ( { If Black plays } 11... Ne8 { , attempting to create counterplay immediately with ...f7-f5 , then } 12. O-O-O $5 { gives a promising position after } Kh7 13. h4 Nc5 14. h5 g5 15. g4 $16 { when White had a huge positional advantage in Zaja – Zufic, Bosnia 2011. } ) 
12. Nc1 ( { If } 12. O-O-O h5 ( 12. Nc1 b5 $5 ) 
13. Kb1 b5 { and Black will have sufficient counterplay on the queenside. } ) 
 ( { Again } 12. Bxh6 { is met by } Nxe4 $1 { . } ) 
b5 { This is a double-edged decision; it gains some space on the queenside, but it weakens the c6-square. } ( { I would have preferred } 12... Kh7 $5 13. Be2 Ne8 14. O-O f5 { with a playable position for Black. } ) 
13. Be2 Nb6 14. O-O { White has decided to castle kingside, and Black has no time to lose – he must play ...f7-f5 as soon as possible. } Kh7 { Protecting the h6-pawn, preparing ...Nh5 and ...f7-f5. } 15. b4 { Fixing the b5-pawn to create a weakness on the queenside with a2-a4. } ( { An interesting option was } 15. b3 $5 { covering the c4-square and preparing Nd3-b4-c6. } Bb7 ( { If Black simply proceeds with his kingside plans with } 15. b4 Nh5 { then the manoeuvre } 16. Nd3 f5 17. Nb4 { causes him serious trouble. For example: } Nf4 18. Nc6 Qc7 19. Rac1 $16 ) 
16. Nd3 Rc8 17. Rfc1 $14 { With a comfortable advantage, followed by a2-a4 weakening Black’s queenside. } ) 
Nh5 16. a4 Nc4 { A necessary decision. } ( { If } 16... bxa4 17. Nxa4 Nxa4 18. Rxa4 $16 { Black’s queenside is weak and easily accessible to White’s forces. } ) 
17. Bxc4 bxc4 { This is a good moment to weigh up the situation. White has managed to create a serious weakness on the queenside – the c4-pawn. Meanwhile, Black is ready to embark on a kingside attack with ...f7-f5. Chances are somewhat favourable to White, but the situation is far from clear, which is generally what Black players hope for in King’s Indian structures. } 18. N1e2 { Protecting the f4-square. } ( { Attempting to capture the c4-pawn immediately with } 18. Qa2 { is met by } Nf4 $1 19. Qxc4 Qg5 20. Bxf4 exf4 { when Black has excellent compensation for the pawn, due to his strong bishops. } ) 
