 ( { PH: Here the computer line is quite impressive: } 19. b5 f4 20. Bf2 g5 21. h3 Nf6 { and it looks like White is ripe for the slaughter on the kingside with ...h5 and ...g4 coming, but: } 22. b6 h5 23. a5 g4 24. Bh4 { Just ignoring Black's initiative! } gxh3 25. gxh3 Bxh3 26. Rf2 { and with Rh2 coming, White is incredibly solid and Black's extra pawn is meaningless. } ) 
gxf5 20. f4 $6 { After this move the position is simply unclear. Note how the attention is now far away from Black’s weakened queenside. } ( { As Rogozenko suggests, } 20. Qa2 { was a better continuation, but after } Qf6 21. Qxc4 Qg6 { Black can grab some compensation by creating tactical threats such as } 22. Qd3 Bd7 23. Rab1 Rg8 { . And now the careless: } 24. Kh1 $2 ( { instead } 24. g3 { allows White to retain a small edge } ) 
 { is met by: } e4 $1 25. Qd2 ( { or } 25. fxe4 Bxc3 $19 ) 
f4 $1 26. Bxf4 Bxc3 27. Nxc3 Nxf4 $17 ) 
Bd7 21. Rab1 Re8 22. Kh1 Rb8 { All Black’s pieces are doing something for his position. } 23. Rf3 Nf6 { Aiming to play ...Ng4. } 24. h3 Nh5 { Now that the g3-square is weakened, the knight returns to h5. Black’s position is easier to conduct. } 25. Bg1 $2 { Too slow. White is not really going anywhere, and Black now has a free hand to move around the board and create threats against White’s kingside. } ( { Stronger was } 25. b5 $5 { creating a passed pawn to distract Black’s attention: } axb5 26. axb5 Rb7 27. b6 Qf6 { with a complex position. } ) 
