Nxf4 30. Bxf4 Qxf4 { when White has no compensation for the pawn. } ) 
29. Qf1 Rgb8 { Now the b4-pawn is lost. } 30. g3 Qd8 31. b5 axb5 32. axb5 Nf6 33. g4 Bxb5 34. Qg2 ( 34. gxf5 { doesn’t help: } Be8 $1 35. Rxb7 Rxb7 36. Ng3 Bf7 37. Qxc4 Qa5 { Winning the d5-pawn and later the game. } ) 
Bd7 $19 { White has no compensation for the pawn, and the rest is relatively simple. } 35. Rg1 fxg4 36. hxg4 Nxg4 37. Rg3 Qh4 38. Rxg4 Bxg4 39. Qxg4 Qxg4 40. Rxg4 { White has gained two pieces for a rook, but after: } Rb2 $1 { White’s pieces are tied up and soon he will lose material. } 41. f5 R8b3 { Threatening ...Bxc3. } 42. f6 ( { If } 42. Rg3 { then: } Be5 43. Re3 Rxc3 $1 44. Nxc3 ( { or } 44. Rxc3 Bxc3 45. Nxc3 e3 $19 ) 
Rxh2 $19 ) 
Bxf6 43. Nxe4 Bh8 44. N4g3 c3 { It is amusing that Ponomariov never found the time to capture the weak c4-pawn, and now this very pawn decides the game for Black. } 45. Rc4 Be5 46. Nc1 Rb1 47. Ne2 R3b2 48. Bg1 Rc2 ( { White resigns. He is in semi-zugzwang, since } 48... Rc2 48. Re4 { loses to } Rd2 { followed by ...c3-c2. } ) 
