 { Dalibor Stojanovic – Ding Liren, Istanbul Olympiad 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates Black’s effective queenside play once the position is somewhat simplified. 2. Later in the game we see the battle between White’s light-squared bishop and Black’s dark-squared bishop, where Black executes the strong ...Bf6-d8-b6 manoeuvre and manages to win convincingly. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. Bd3 Nf6 6. O-O d6 7. c4 b6 8. Nc3 Bb7 9. Qe2 g6 10. Bc2 Bg7 11. Rd1 Nbd7 12. Nf3 Qc7 13. Bf4 e5 14. Bg5 h6 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. Ba4+ Ke7 { We have reached a very interesting position, corresponding to the Najdorf Type II. As the reader may recall, one of the key issues in that structure is the control of the d5-square, thus we know White must be doing okay at the very least. However, comparing this position with the game Tiviakov – Anand from that chapter, we see a crucial difference: White has the wrong bishop. Typically, the light-squared bishop can be very effective on b3 if the c-pawn is still on c2; as an example, take the game Almasi – Aagaard from that chapter. In the present case though, the light-squared bishop has few prospects as it is blocked by the c4-pawn, which means Black is doing fine. } 17. Nd5+ $2 { This is a very unfortunate positional decision; the kind of mistake I hope you will not make after reading this book. White chooses a very bad occasion to go into the structure studied in this chapter. } ( { Playing this position in the spirit of a Najdorf Type II yields only equality, for example: } 17. Bc2 Rhc8 18. Bd3 Kf8 19. Nd2 h5 20. Nf1 Bh6 { with level chances. } ) 
 ( { But instead, White could have prepared } . Nd5 { , hoping to obtain this chapter’s structure under favourable circumstances. I believe the strongest move was: } 17. Rac1 Rac8 18. Rc2 ( 18. Nd5 $2 { is premature due to: } Nxd5 19. cxd5 $2 Qxc1 $17 ) 
Rhd8 $2 { Permitting White’s positional threat. } ( 18. Nd5+ Qxc4 $2 { loses to } 19. Nd5 Bxd5 20. Rxc4 Bxc4 21. Qe3 ) 
 ( { Correct is } 18. Nd5+ Qb8 { anticipating } 19. Nd5 { , and now } 19. b4 $5 { ⩲ gives a small advantage to White. } ) 
19. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 20. cxd5 Qb8 21. Bc6 $16 { White invades down the c-file, while Black’s dark-squared bishop is far away from the action. } ) 
Nxd5 18. cxd5 { And this is a key position to analyze. The reader should ask: what is the difference between this position and Wojtaszek – Swiercz? Black is not well prepared to pursue counterplay with ...f7-f5, but under these conditions he does not even have to. The main point is that there are only two minor pieces on the board, and in particular White is missing his typically strong dark-squared bishop. Black’s pieces have sufficient space to manoeuvre, thus the position is roughly equal, or even slightly preferable for Black due to the awkward location of White’s light-squared bishop. } b5 ( { Another alternative was } 18... Rhc8 $5 { securing control of the c-file. It is possible that Black wanted to prevent } 19. Bc6 $5 { getting rid of the bad light-squared bishop } ( { but not } 18... b5 18. Rac1 $2 Qxc1 20. Rxc1 Rxc1 21. Ne1 Rac8 $17 ) 
 ( 18... b5 18. Ne1 $6 { is met by } Qc4 $1 { ⩱ } ) 
Bxc6 20. dxc6 Qxc6 21. Qd3 { when White has reasonable compensation for the pawn, but it is Black who can play for an advantage. } ) 
 ( { PH: After } 18... Rhc8 { the computer likes } 19. Rd3 $5 { , from where the rook can come either to c3 or to the f-file: } b5 20. Bd1 a5 21. h4 $5 { , intending h5, and if } h5 22. Ng5 { , intending Rf3.. } ) 
19. Bb3 ( { The alternative was } 19. Bc2 { aiming to play Bd3 followed by a2-a4, and then } Bc8 $1 { is a good option, relocating the bishop and keeping an eye on an eventual ...f7-f5 . } 20. Bd3 Qb6 21. a4 Bd7 22. axb5 axb5 { Black has nothing to fear. } ) 
Rhc8 20. Ne1 ( { PH: Too passive. } 20. h4 $1 { and White is just better. } ) 
Qb6 21. a4 bxa4 22. Bxa4 Kf8 { This gives White an interesting opportunity. } ( { More precise was } 22... a5 23. Nc2 Kf8 { transposing into the game. } ) 
23. Nc2 ( { Somewhat better was } 23. Rd3 { trying to disrupt Black’s queenside play to some extent: } a5 24. Rb3 Qa7 25. Bb5 { with approximately level chances. } ) 
a5 24. Qe3 Rc5 25. Ne1 Ba6 26. Bc6 { The bishop does not do much on c6, but the real problem with this move is a small tactical detail which becomes clear after move 28. } ( { Better was } 26. b3 Rb8 { when Black’s position is slightly better, but White is very likely to hold since Black’s dark-squared bishop cannot enter the fray yet. } ) 
Rb8 27. Nd3 Bxd3 $1 { This is a strong decision, giving up the pair of bishops at the right moment. } ( { Another option was } 27... Rc2 $5 28. Qxb6 Rxb6 29. h3 { but White seems to hold well enough. } ) 
28. Qxd3 ( { And not } 28. Rxd3 $2 Qxb2 $17 { winning a pawn. } ) 
Bf6 $1 { ⩱ This is the key to Black’s previous move. White’s queen has moved away from e3, so the h6-pawn no longer needs protection. I cannot overstate how much I like this move; the idea is to bring the dark-squared bishop into the game via d8-b6, which is similar to the game Polgar – Dominguez from Chapter 8. We have reached a very unpleasant position for White. This is one of those cases where the presence of opposite-coloured bishops does not help the defending side. White will be dominated on the dark squares while the bishop on c6 is basically doing nothing. } ( { But certainly not } 28... Qxb2 $4 29. Rdb1 ) 
29. g3 h5 ( { Again } 29... Qxb2 $4 { loses to } 30. Rab1 { . } ) 
 ( { An alternative was } 29... Qb3 $5 { (threatening ...Qxd3 and then ...Rxb2) and now } 30. Qxb3 Rxb3 31. Rd2 Bd8 $5 { ⩱ is similar to the game } ( { instead, gaining a pawn with } 31... Bg5 32. Re2 Rc1 33. Rxc1 Bxc1 34. Ba4 Rxb2 35. Rxb2 Bxb2 { leads to a drawish endgame } ) 
 ) 
30. Rdb1 Qb4 ( { Stronger was: } 30... Qb3 31. Qxb3 Rxb3 32. Ra3 Rb4 $17 ) 
31. Ra4 $6 { An imprecise defence. } ( { Slightly better was } 31. Qe2 Kg7 32. Ba4 Bg5 33. Kg2 Qd2 $5 34. Qxd2 Bxd2 $15 { which is similar to the game, but White is better prepared to defend his weaknesses. } ) 
Qb3 32. Qxb3 Rxb3 33. Kg2 ( { A better defence was } 33. Ra2 Bd8 34. Ba4 Rb4 35. f3 f5 $1 36. exf5 gxf5 37. Rd1 Bb6 $17 { when White’s position is very uncomfortable due to the weak pawns on b2 and d5 . } ) 
Bd8 { Threatening ...Rc2 and ...Bb6. } 34. Ra3 Rb4 35. Ra4 { Unfortunately for White, there is nothing better than this move, and now: } Rxa4 36. Bxa4 Rc4 37. Bb5 Rxe4 $19 { This position and the rest of the game are very similar to Polgar – Dominguez from Chapter 8. In fact, it is the same structure only with an extra pawn on a5. The rest of the game is a pleasing illustration of imposing the advantage with opposite-coloured bishops. } 38. b3 Rb4 39. Bd3 Rd4 40. Bc4 Bb6 { Threatening ...Rd2. } 41. Rb2 Rd1 42. Re2 f5 43. h4 e4 { The reader should notice how Black has expanded with ...f7-f5 and ...e5-e4, exactly as Dominguez did in the game mentioned above. } 44. Rb2 Kg7 { Black threatens to follow up with ...Kf6-e5 and ...f5-f4. } 45. f4 { Preventing the potential ...f5-f4, but now the e4-pawn is passed and very strong. White’s position is hopeless, but the rest of the game is even nicer. } ( { White cannot wait and allow Black’s plan with } 45. Rc2 Kf6 46. Ra2 Ke5 47. Rc2 f4 $1 { (threatening ...f4-f3) } 48. gxf4 Kxf4 49. Re2 Bd8 { and the h4-pawn is lost. } ) 
Rg1+ 46. Kh2 Rc1 47. Kg2 Bc5 48. Ra2 Bb4 { White has been locked up. } 49. Bb5 Rc3 50. Bc4 Kf6 51. Kh2 Ke7 { The king marches decisively to the queenside. } 52. Kg2 Kd8 53. Kh2 Rxc4 $1 { A very nice final blow – even my engine does not believe it at first – but Black is completely winning after this sacrifice. } ( { Of course } 53... Kc7 $5 { followed by ...Kb6-c5 and ...a5-a4 is also winning. } ) 
54. bxc4 Kc7 55. Kg2 Kb6 56. Kf2 ( 56. Ra1 { doesn’t help: } Kc5 57. Rc1 a4 58. Kf2 a3 59. Ke3 Ba5 { Followed by ...Kb4 winning. } ) 
Kc5 57. Rc2 a4 ( { White resigns. White is unable to prevent the promotion of the a-pawn after: } 57... a4 57. Ke2 a3 59. Ke3 Ba5 $1 { Clearing the b4-square for the king: } 60. Ke2 Kb4 61. Kd1 Kb3 { Final remarks 1. The resemblance between this game and Polgar – Dominguez from Chapter 8 is astonishing, and the reader is encouraged to revisit that game. This similarity only reinforces how similar pawn structures have similar plans and strategic elements. 2. White was unable to gradually increase his queenside predominance (as often occurs in this structure) because two minor pieces were already off the board on move 18, and because his light-squared bishop was of little use. 3. Black’s most essential move was 28...Bf6, bringing the bishop into the game via d8-b6. This thematic manoeuvre is something to remember, as the bishop became much stronger than White’s bishop on c6. } ) 

 0-1

